  "response": "The dispute is adjudicated by asking what a given replication design is able to distinguish: a genuinely absent effect, on the one hand, from an effect that depends on conditions the original report never specified, on the other. The Reproducibility Project: Psychology attempted replications of 100 studies drawn from three psychology journals, and the arithmetic of its outcome admits of exactly one reading: either fewer than half of those 100 replications produced significant effects in the original direction, or at least half of them did, and precisely one of the two tallies is the correct description.\n\nThirty-six percent of the Reproducibility Project: Psychology replications produced statistically significant results in the same direction as the originals. After that figure entered the literature, Gilbert and colleagues published a commentary in Science in 2016 arguing that infidelities in replication protocols depressed the measured replication rate. Anderson and colleagues replied that the critique depended on unsupported statistical assumptions, a reply that rules out the attribution of the low rate to protocol infidelity: the infidelity diagnosis and the demonstration that it rests on unsupported assumptions cannot both stand. The 2016 commentary nevertheless had a lasting methodological consequence, since it drove the construction of multi-laboratory replication designs, which estimate between-site heterogeneity in order to test whether hidden moderators are operating.\n\nHaving tested 28 published findings in more than 60 laboratories around the world, Many Labs 2 thereby confirms that 14 of those 28 examined findings obtained support. Many Labs 2 concluded that cultural differences between participant samples accounted for most of the failed replications. The partial support recovered across those laboratories indicates that some of the original findings that failed to replicate were false positives. The attribution of most non-replications to cultural variation between samples, for its part, confirms that some replication attempts differed from the original studies in procedural details that affected the outcome. Spuriousness in an original result and procedural divergence in its replication are therefore not rival diagnoses between which a choice must be forced: at least one of the two holds in any given failure, and in a substantial share of cases both hold together.\n\nDiagnosis in practice consequently rests on design rather than on dispute, through preregistered protocols vetted in advance by the original authors, many-site sampling, and explicit decomposition of variance into within-site and between-site components. The 2019 National Academies consensus report on reproducibility and replicability concluded that non-replication can stem both from deficiencies in the original study and from unrecognized differences in study conditions, even though the Registered Replication Report on ego depletion recovered a large effect matching the original estimate. The verdict of the decade is accordingly not the triumph of one explanation over the other but a division of labor between them, with the burden falling on whoever posits a moderator to name that moderator and measure its influence.",
